{
  "image_name": "2f5d6097b95a3ab2860be76dd152805c.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how genetic and epigenetic abnormalities may contribute to fibroblast-like synoviocytes in rheumatoid arthritis. The top portion highlights somatic mutations arising in both mitochondrial and nuclear DNA. Examples shown include changes in vimentin, TP53, and RAF1, which are linked visually to increased ACPAs, reduced apoptosis, and enhanced MAPK signaling. These mutations appear to be promoted by inflammatory injury, including reactive oxygen and nitrogen species, together with impaired DNA repair, suggesting a feedback loop between inflammation and genomic damage.\n\nThe lower portion focuses on epigenetic regulation across chromatin and DNA. It contrasts condensed, inactive chromatin with relaxed, active chromatin, emphasizing histone marks that favor activation versus repression. DNA methylation at CpG sites is shown as another key layer, with a distinct methylation signature affecting pathways related to extracellular matrix interactions, inflammation, proliferation, and differentiation. MicroRNAs provide a third epigenetic mechanism: altered miRNA expression is linked to changes in target mRNA degradation or inhibition. The examples shown suggest decreased miR-34a may lessen apoptosis through XIAP, while increased miR-203 may promote inflammatory mediators such as IL-6 and MMP1. Environmental and inherited influences appear to feed into these epigenetic changes.",
